.TH "GYRO" "1" "Nov 2026" "Gyro Hosting" "gyro manual"

.SH NAME
gyro-container-log-retention-set - Change the log package


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBgyro container log-retention set  [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Change the log package

.PP
\fBdays\fR 7 | 30 | 90 | 365 and \fBgb\fR (monthly size) from \fBGET .../logs/plans\fR; \fB{"free": true}\fR goes back to the free package. A retention SHORTER than the current one deletes the older logs permanently (\fBdata_loss\fR, \fBdeletes_logs_older_than_days\fR and a \fBwarning\fR text in the answer; \fBGET .../logs/plans\fR marks such packages \fBdata_loss\fR). Every switch from free to a paid package is billed at least 6 hours (\fBminimum_hours\fR); going back to free earlier charges the remaining hours at once (\fBminimum_completion_hours\fR, shown as "minimum completion" in the hourly detail and CSV, like an additional IPv4). A paid package is billed hourly from the prepaid balance (billing line "Log retention") and needs \fB"confirm": true\fR\&. 409/422 when the platform has no room for the package. Logs are what your containers write to stdout / stderr. Every query is built by the platform with your environment fixed; there is no raw query language. \fBFree package\fP: 7 days, 5 GB per month. Paid packages (30 / 90 / 365 days x 10 / 50 / 100 / 300 GB per month, prepaid hourly; \fBGET /cloud/containers/{id}/logs/plans\fR) keep logs longer. Over the monthly size there is no extra charge: the stored volume is capped at GB x days / 30, the oldest logs are deleted earlier and you get the \fBlog_quota\fR notification at 80 % and 100 %. When the environment is cancelled its logs are deleted at once. Best effort service, no compensation for lost log lines.

.PP
API: PUT /cloud/containers/{id}/logs/retention (operationId setContainerLogPackage, scope services_write)


